  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "note_shortest",
      "text": "Trace finds the SHORTEST chain of connections, not the most meaningful one.\n\nbooks -> customers has two routes: through reviews (2 hops) and through order_items -> orders (3 hops). Trace always returns the 2-hop one — 'who reviewed this book' — even when 'who bought this book' (one hop longer) is the question you actually meant.\n\nHop count is not a cost model. Read the hop list before you trust the JOIN.",
      "position": { "x": 960, "y": 0 },
      "width": 300,
      "height": 210,
      "color": "yellow"
    },
    {
      "id": "note_dependency",
      "text": "authors -> reviews is a dependency, not a foreign key (see its note in the inspector). Trace still walks it: a path that needs it comes out as CROSS JOIN plus a comment, because there is no shared column to write an ON clause from.\n\nTry tracing authors -> customers. It is shorter through this dependency and reviews than through books and reviews, so the generated query mixes a real JOIN with a CROSS JOIN.",
      "position": { "x": 960, "y": 520 },
      "width": 300,
      "height": 220,
      "color": "yellow"
    }
  ]
